    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "...that there's enough amino acids present to start building, and this signaling is thought to be mediated through a complex interplay of leucine receptors and downstream signaling molecules. Specifically, leucine binds to the mTOR complex through the leucine-sensitive amino acid transporter (LSAT), which is a specific transporter that recognizes leucine and other BCAAs. Once bound, leucine triggers a conformational change in the mTOR complex, activating its kinase activity and leading to the phosphorylation and activation of downstream targets.\n\nRegarding the threshold for excess leucine, research suggests that high levels of leucine can indeed lead to a decrease in its effectiveness as a mTOR activator. This is thought to occur when the mTOR pathway becomes saturated with leucine, leading to a decrease in its ability to activate downstream targets. At this point, excess leucine may be broken down for energy instead of being used for protein synthesis.\n\nStudies have shown that the optimal dose of leucine for mTOR activation is around 2-3 mmol/L, which is roughly equivalent to 10-15 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight. At this dose, leucine is able to activate m",
